      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexander A. Belavin",
        "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov",
        "Alexander M. Polyakov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Two-dimensional conformal field theory gave a powerful, general framework for critical phenomena and connected statistical physics with quantum field theory. Its conceptual reach and lasting use make it exceptionally Nobel-worthy.",
        "attribution": "Belavin, Polyakov, and A. Zamolodchikov established the foundational framework and its applications to critical systems; the proposed trio represents the central early contribution.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been tested and extended across many decades and systems. Its importance is well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The broad development of conformal field theory involved later contributors, so the precise boundary of the prize contribution needs care."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B048",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Mehran Kardar",
        "Yi-Cheng Zhang",
        "Kazumasa A. Takeuchi"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The KPZ framework identified a central universality class for nonequilibrium growth and inspired a broad body of theory and experiment. Its combination of a simple mechanism and universal predictions is compelling.",
        "attribution": "Kardar and Zhang are credited for the foundational theoretical formulation; Takeuchi's precise experiments provided unusually direct confirmation of KPZ scaling and fluctuations.",
        "maturity": "The theory has been established for decades, and later experiments have tested its characteristic scaling and distributions in multiple settings.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed discovery omits other foundational contributors to the equation, making a complete attribution difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B061",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory made strongly correlated electron problems tractable through a clean, broadly useful mapping to an effective local problem. It has substantially shaped modern condensed-matter theory.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar were central in developing and establishing the method and its applications; the work was distributed across a closely connected community.",
        "maturity": "The method has been tested against exact limits and experiments and remains an active standard tool, with its value well demonstrated.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The method's successes are strongest in settings where locality is a good approximation; its limits should be distinguished from the discovery itself."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Andrea J. Liu",
        "Sidney R. Nagel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The jamming transition organized a wide range of observations in disordered materials around a general transition between rigid and flowing states. It is a strong candidate for a prize recognizing a new physical framework.",
        "attribution": "Liu and Nagel were central in formulating and promoting the jamming picture and its connections across systems.",
        "maturity": "The concept has been extensively studied in experiments, simulations, and theory over several decades, though its relation to distinct glass and yielding transitions remains under discussion.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The scope and universality of a single jamming framework across different materials remain debated."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B022",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "John Toner",
        "Yuhai Tu",
        "Tamás Vicsek"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The theory of flocking showed how collective motion and long-range order can arise in nonequilibrium systems, giving active matter a distinctive statistical-physics foundation.",
        "attribution": "Vicsek's model and simulations helped establish the phenomenon; Toner and Tu developed influential theory explaining its large-scale behavior. The three contributions are complementary.",
        "maturity": "The framework has motivated extensive theory and experiments, but active matter remains a developing field with varied systems and mechanisms.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The credited discovery combines model, theory, and a broad research area, so the prize scope and division of credit would need precise definition."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Maldacena",
        "Edward Witten",
        "Leonard Susskind"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Gauge/gravity duality is a profound conceptual connection that has influenced quantum gravity, quantum field theory, and statistical physics. Its reach is exceptional.",
        "attribution": "Maldacena's specific duality conjecture is the pivotal step; Witten and Susskind made major contributions to its context, development, and interpretation.",
        "maturity": "The mathematical structure has been developed in depth and applied fruitfully, but the duality lacks direct empirical confirmation as a description of nature.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The physical status and experimental testability of the conjecture remain unresolved.",
          "The proposed laureate group spans distinct contributions to a broad intellectual program."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B013",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks combine major advances in precision measurement with a route to unprecedentedly accurate timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye made independent, complementary contributions to the clock concept and its experimental realization.",
        "maturity": "The technology is mature enough for striking demonstrations and applications, although its broader metrological and fundamental impact continues to grow.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has many important technical contributors, and the boundary between the original invention and subsequent clock development requires care."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B028",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Allan H. MacDonald",
        "Rafi Bistritzer",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene revealed that moiré flat bands can host striking correlated phases in a highly tunable setting. It opened a major direction in quantum materials research.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald predicted the key flat-band mechanism; Jarillo-Herrero's group demonstrated the correlated superconducting and insulating phenomena that made it experimentally compelling.",
        "maturity": "The discovery has prompted rapid and extensive experimental work, but the range of phases, mechanisms, and reproducibility across devices is still being resolved.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relationship between observed phases and the original flat-band mechanism remains an active research question."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
